hi there  hello steve kaufman you know you get the  double hello from me because i have to  turn on my other  recording setup for the for the podcast  today i want to talk a little bit about  a man  by the name of daniel tamet  and on my blog i will put a link to an  interview that he has done in german  another one he's done in english  he also has a website he is a savant  in other words he is apparently one of  50 people in the world who have  a certain set of unique  capabilities he is able to calculate  pi you know we all had to  in our maths at school pi was two point  i don't know what it is but he can count  he can remember that down to thousands  of points he can calculate in his mind  he can divide three by 54 and get it  correct down to a 100 or 200 points  behind the decimal i mean he's an  amazing brain  and he can learn a language in a week  and so in this particular interview one  of the interviews with a german  television station  he speaks in german and apparently he  had a teacher there and he just  learned german for a week and he speaks  it  phenomenally  so and he has a language learning system  and so forth  so i thought it'd be interesting to talk  about this issue and and i was so  inspired by it that i went out and i  bought his book which is a bestseller  here embracing the wide sky by daniel  temet and i've read bits and pieces of  it  and it is amazing  i mean it shows us the potential of our  brain  if our brain were wired just a little  bit differently  we would be more like him maybe  and he claims that we can all do much  better than we do in terms of using our  brain and perhaps that's true  but i sort of focused on the things that  he had to say about language learning  and i want to talk a little bit about  them here  the first thing that he said which i  firmly believe is that the brain is a  learning machine it cannot help but  learn and this was a major theme  of manfred spitzer in his book learning  fortune you know brain research and the  school of life  our brains will learn so we got to make  sure that we help them learn and don't  over complicate the teaching  let the brains learn so i certainly  agreed with that and he also makes the  point that once we've learned something  and we learn it and store it in  different parts of the brain that helps  us learn other things if we learn the  word for dog he makes the example you  learn the word for dog or for cat it'll  be easier than to learn  you know the word for another animal the  concept of a four-legged animal now  we're sort of building on the knowledge  that we already have and i think that's  very important i notice it all the time  when you start a new language the more  you know the more things you've managed  to puzzle through the easier it is to  add more things to your knowledge base  if you know nothing everything is  confusing and the brain i believe sort  of picks out those things that are easy  is for you to learn starts to put those  pieces of the puzzle together and then  it becomes easier to learn other things  and it's futile to try to force yourself  to learn  things that are more difficult before  you had some of the other pieces  put together for you  daniel temet also stresses the  importance of emotion  if you can be emotionally involved with  what you're learning about it uh  it's it's very positive it sends you  know positive chemicals charging around  your brain i mean i'm not a scientist  motivation is extremely important  he doesn't talk so much about  attentiveness but it's something that i  believe in it's something that manfred  spitzer mentions  it's something that we've tried to build  into our  learning methodology in other words it's  not just that you have a interesting  content to learn from it's not just that  you're motivated to learn but you also  need to do things to make your brain a  little more attentive now for daniel  tameth that's not an issue because he  just vacuum cleans  all this information and he's able to  get at it and retrieve it  whereas most of us absorb all this  information  and we've listened to hours of this  language we've read it and we can't find  the words they're in our brains  somewhere we can't sort of retrieve them  and i didn't see anything in his book  that suggested that he had  some easy answers as to how we could  improve our ability to retrieve  information we certain that we certainly  have  haven't have absorbed  he has some things that i find difficult  to accept  he sort of says it helps him because  like he he identifies remembers numbers  based on  associating them with color and stuff  like that i can't do that maybe some  people can  and he identifies certain sounds you  know sounds that begin with kr are all  long or short or you know he identifies  the fact that there is a uh  an identification  between what a word sounds like and what  it means and that he claims that can  help us  my feeling is that if that helps us at  all it's happening subconsciously i've  never been consciously aware of  remembering a word because it sounded  like what i thought that you know  meaning should  sound like  uh so i didn't really buy that concept  but for those people who are helped by  it that's fine  he makes the point that a lot of the  difficulty he says and i firmly agree  with this he says that this whole  critical period in terms of language  learning really only applies to the  first language and it doesn't apply to a  second language  and what happens is that the first  language gets kind of locked into your  brain  and it then becomes a filter and it  warps  your attempt to learn other languages so  you tend to pronounce the second  language  in a way that is corrupted by your first  language you tend to use expressions  from your first language so to that  extent having you know perhaps during  your critical period as a child you've  you've fixed on certain patterns  and therefore that influences your  perception of the second language so  that's true  and that's why it's so important to  leave your first language behind  i refer to my first language only in  dictionaries i like to see the new  meaning quickly in my own language  even though i know that that meaning is  very incomplete and that i will get a  better and better sense of the meaning  of that word as i see it in more and  more context but i like that but after  that i don't like to hear english on  audio tape  recordings i want to get away from my  native language i want to get away from  my native culture i want to get  try to project myself and imagine myself  as russian chinese japanese french  whatever language i'm into  the other thing that you know he says he  can learn a language in a week i don't  know how it's possible to do that  uh there are the big thing and here he  agrees you know he makes the point that  which i have always said you know this  whole myth that you if you have a  thousand words you can communicate in a  language  yeah and he demonstrates how in fact  those very common words really don't get  you the meaning in other words all the  meaning is in the 20  less frequent words i you he go that  there then in out from two by whatever  that's not going to get to the meaning  you need a lot of words i'm experiencing  this now in russia i have spent so much  time on russian i have listened to  thousands of hours of russian i've read  lots of russian i have saved  you know 20 000 links in link i am  theoretically i know 45 000 words  and as i read  stuff they're still key words that i  don't understand and my for me the most  the biggest challenge is to get to where  some of these you know the key word that  keeps on showing up that this is the  subject of this article or this point in  the novel  i'm missing it and so there are just a  lot of words i don't know how a person  can even be exposed to all the words he  needs to know in one week let alone  remember them let alone produce them i  can't  this whole business of producing which  is another subject that i should get  into i mean even in terms of getting  things right grammatically uh initially  i couldn't understand the  the grammatical explanations in russian  i now understand them  i now am able to relate them to things  that i'm used to seeing in russian i  even now know that this is the data this  is the janitor this is the instrumental  singular plural feminine mask or  whatever but to produce it  it's still very very difficult doesn't  matter how often i read the explanation  how often i look at the table  and i save them now in link and that's  helping me and i tag them and i review  them but when i go to speak  to try to actually get the instrumental  get that whether it's a ooh or whatever  it might be get the right ending is very  very difficult so unless your daniel  tenet where everything you absorb into  your brain all the input you get is  available to you because you've got this  tremendous control of what's going on in  your brain  uh i don't think one week is is feasible  and i think you're stuck with a longer  process and the key thing is to make it  continually enjoyable and that i must  admit my russian learning remains  tremendously enjoyable i'm very  satisfied with the progress i've made  even though it's taken me almost three  years where daniel tamet does it  in one week so go and see his website

